Title: BricsCAD refuses to LOAD a file containing a form it executes happily
Status: REOPENED 2026-09-06 -- the named form was disproved
Found: 2026-08-31, chasing probes-suite-crashes-bricscad

THE SHAPE OF IT

probes/sources/probe-foreach-scope.lsp contained, inside one function:

    (list (foreach e (list 1 2 3) (* e 10))
          (foreach e nil 99))

and earlier, in the same function, `(foreach e (list 1 2 3))' with no
body. BricsCAD would not LOAD the file -- on macOS and on Windows,
before a single probe ran, with no diagnostic reaching the results
file: the process simply sat until the harness's 180-second timeout.

All three of those forms EXECUTE CORRECTLY in BricsCAD. The suite now
holds them as strings and READs them when the case runs, and BricsCAD
answers every one -- including returning (1 2 3) for the body-less
FOREACH, which is a divergence worth its own ticket
(foreach-empty-body-return-diverges.issue).

So the rejection is in whatever reads a file being LOADED, and not in
the evaluator. A form BricsCAD will run is a form BricsCAD will not
read from a file.

WHICH FORM, EXACTLY, IS STILL UNKNOWN

The per-form split (PROBE_SPLIT_SUITES) narrowed it to ONE FUNCTION --
form 10 of 13, CAD-PROBE-FOREACH--RETURN-VALUE -- which is what finally
ended the guessing. It did not narrow it further, because all three
candidates sat in that one function:

  - FOREACH in EXPRESSION POSITION, as an argument to LIST
  - FOREACH over an EMPTY LIST
  - FOREACH with NO BODY
  - or only some combination of them

THE EXPERIMENT IS NOW WRITTEN -- probes/sources/probe-load-refusal.lsp,
plus probe:load-refusal:bricscad:{macos,windows}. It holds the three
candidates as three separate top-level forms, written OUT rather than
hidden in strings, because loading them is the question. Run alone and
SPLIT, each becomes its own file with its own marker, so the last
marker names the form BricsCAD would not read.

Three things had to be true for that to be safe, and now are:

  - QUARANTINE. The suite is marked `quarantine' in the manifest, so a
    default `make probe' SKIPS it and only PROBE_SUITES=load-refusal
    runs it. Without that, adding this file would have turned every
    ordinary BricsCAD probe run into a hang -- the trap this whole
    ticket is about, re-laid by the ticket's own fix.
  - PROBE_TIMEOUT. The harness's 180 s is fine for a suite that works;
    an experiment that EXPECTS a hang runs at 45 s, because the Windows
    runner is serial and nothing else should queue behind a deliberate
    hang.
  - allow_failure. A failed job here is a RESULT, not a regression.

Verified on the clautolisp baseline both ways: a default run produces
265 results and ZERO from this suite, and the named run loads all four
forms with their markers and answers all three cases `ok'.

ROUND 1 -- 2026-09-02, BricsCAD on macOS. THE CULPRIT IS NAMED.

    ((KIND . "step") (AT . "loaded probe-load-refusal.lsp form 1 ...")
                     (VALUE . "CAD-PROBE-LR--EXPRESSION-POSITION"))

and nothing after it. Form 1 LOADED -- its marker carries the function
it defined. Form 2's marker never appears.

  (list (foreach e (list 1 2 3) (* e 10)))   LOADED   -- innocent
  (foreach e nil 99)                         REFUSED  -- the culprit
  (foreach e (list 1 2 3))                   not reached

BRICSCAD WILL NOT READ A FOREACH WHOSE LIST ARGUMENT IS A LITERAL NIL,
from a file it is loading. It executes the same form perfectly well
when the form is READ at run time -- that is how the empty-list return
value was measured, and it is nil there, as in AutoCAD.

FOREACH IN EXPRESSION POSITION IS INNOCENT, which is worth stating
because it was the candidate I would have bet on: a loop used as an
argument looks like the unusual thing, and it is not.

ONE RUN FINDS AT MOST ONE CULPRIT, and it finds the FIRST -- a refusal
hangs the engine, so everything after it is never attempted. The
suite's form ORDER is therefore part of the experiment, not
housekeeping: the body-less form, still unknown, now goes FIRST and the
known culprit goes LAST. That is written at the head of the suite so
the next person does not reorder it tidily and lose the design.

WHAT THIS MEANS IN PRACTICE, and it is not nothing: `(foreach x nil
...)' is what a program contains when the list argument is a constant
that happens to be empty -- a degenerate case a generator or a macro
expansion produces easily. Such a file loads in AutoCAD and in
clautolisp and DOES NOT LOAD in BricsCAD, with no diagnostic: the
process sits there. Anyone shipping AutoLISP to both hosts should know
it, which is why this ticket stays open until the third candidate is
settled and the whole thing can be written up as a portability note.

ROUND 2 -- same day, body-less form moved first. COMPLETE.

    (step ... form 1 ...) (VALUE . "CAD-PROBE-LR--NO-BODY")
    (step ... form 2 ...) (VALUE . "CAD-PROBE-LR--EXPRESSION-POSITION")

and nothing after. Both loaded; form 3's marker is absent.

  (foreach e (list 1 2 3))                  LOADS    -- innocent
  (list (foreach e (list 1 2 3) (* e 10)))  LOADS    -- innocent
  (foreach e nil 99)                        REFUSED  -- the SOLE culprit

THE ANSWER, in one line: BRICSCAD WILL NOT READ `(foreach VAR nil ...)'
-- a FOREACH WHOSE LIST ARGUMENT IS THE LITERAL NIL -- from a file it
is loading. Nothing else in that function was ever the problem.

Two independent runs agree, and each cleared a different candidate, so
neither verdict rests on a single observation: round 1 cleared
expression position, round 2 cleared the body-less form and confirmed
the culprit by exclusion.

WHAT IS NOT MEASURED, and should not be assumed:
  - whether `(foreach e '() 99)' behaves the same. Only the bare symbol
    NIL was probed. `'()' reads as the same object, so it probably
    does, and `probably' is exactly the word that has been wrong four
    times in this ticket.
  - whether a NON-LITERAL nil is refused -- `(foreach e l 99)' with L
    bound to nil is a run-time value, not something the loader sees, so
    almost certainly not; the whole finding is that this is a READER
    problem.
  - Windows. THREE attempts, none of which produced a Windows result.
    Rounds 1 and 2 had their Windows jobs cancelled by my own pushes;
    the third attempt, where I deliberately held the pushes back, ended
    with all three Windows probe jobs failing as
    `stuck_pending_no_matching_runners' -- the runner dropped
    mid-pipeline, thirteen jobs with it. So the reason is now
    environmental rather than self-inflicted, and GitLab says so
    explicitly rather than leaving a silence.

    The macOS finding is replicated THREE times, and the ORIGINAL
    refusal was seen on both platforms, so the culprit is very likely
    the same on Windows. `Very likely' is not measured, and in this
    ticket that phrase has been wrong four times. RE-RUN THE WINDOWS
    JOB when the runner is up; nothing else is needed, the experiment
    is committed and quarantined.

WHAT IT MEANS IN PRACTICE. `(foreach x nil ...)' is what a program
contains when its list argument is a constant that happens to be empty
-- easily produced by a macro expansion, a generator, or a hand edit
that emptied a literal list. Such a file loads in AutoCAD and in
clautolisp and DOES NOT LOAD in BricsCAD, with no diagnostic at all:
the process sits until something times it out. That is a portability
hazard worth a note in the user manual, and possibly worth a
--dialect bricscad warning, but that is a decision rather than a
finding and is left for pjb.

THE METHOD, which is the reusable part. Four guesses were made about
this file and all four were wrong -- the suite that had loaded fine,
the body-less form, then expression position, and the assumption that a
silence meant the previous load had failed. What settled it was
instrumentation, in three steps: mark every load; split the suite one
form per file so a HANG names the form; and then ORDER the forms so
that the unknown candidate is reached first, because a refusal hangs
the engine and one run can only ever name the FIRST culprit.

That last point is the non-obvious one and it is written at the head of
probe-load-refusal.lsp: the order of the forms in that file is the
experiment's design, not housekeeping.

WHY IT IS NOT URGENT

Nothing in clautolisp depends on the answer. It matters for two
practical reasons and neither is pressing: AutoLISP written for
clautolisp or AutoCAD may fail to LOAD in BricsCAD for reasons no
diagnostic explains, and anyone writing a probe suite will meet this
again. The second is already handled -- the rule is written at the head
of probe-foreach-scope.lsp and the harness has the split.

THE LESSON, which is the durable part

A load that HANGS writes nothing about itself. The marker that would
name the file comes after the load that never returns, and
VL-CATCH-ALL-APPLY cannot catch a process that is not coming back. The
file therefore says only that the PREVIOUS load finished -- a silence.
It was read as evidence twice, and both readings were wrong: first
blaming a suite that had loaded fine, then a form that turned out
innocent. PROBE_SPLIT_SUITES exists so the last marker NAMES the form.


WHAT THE OUTPUT FILE ACTUALLY SAYS -- pjb, 2026-09-06
------------------------------------------------------

pjb: "What do you mean by `(foreach e nil 99) is refused'? I don't see
this form in the probe output file."

He is right, and the distinction is the one this whole ticket is about.
THE FORM APPEARS NOWHERE IN THE RESULTS FILE. The file is four records
and stops:

    ((KIND . "wrapper-start"))
    ((KIND . "load-context") (SECURELOAD . "0"))
    ((KIND . "load") (FILE . "probe-core.lsp") (VALUE . "nil"))
    ((KIND . "step") (AT . "loaded ... form 1 (...-001.lsp)")
                     (VALUE . "CAD-PROBE-LR--NO-BODY"))
    ((KIND . "step") (AT . "loaded ... form 2 (...-002.lsp)")
                     (VALUE . "CAD-PROBE-LR--EXPRESSION-POSITION"))

The identity of the form comes from a DIFFERENT file in the same
artifact -- fragments/probe-load-refusal-003.lsp, written by the split,
which holds (defun cad-probe-lr--empty-list () ... (foreach e nil 99)).

SO WHAT IS ESTABLISHED IS NARROWER THAN "REFUSED":

  - fragments 001 and 002 loaded, and their markers name what they
    defined, so those loads returned;
  - the load of fragment 003 NEVER RETURNED. No marker, and nothing
    afterwards, until the harness timeout;
  - each load is wrapped in VL-CATCH-ALL-APPLY, so a form the engine
    SIGNALLED about would have been caught and recorded WITH its
    marker. There is no marker at all, so this is not a catchable
    refusal -- it is the process not coming back.

"REFUSED" AND "WILL NOT READ" WERE INTERPRETATION, written as finding.
The word "read" in particular claims a decision inside the reader,
which nothing here shows: a hang, a crash, and a modal dialog are all
consistent with the same silence. What IS separately established is
that the evaluator is fine -- the foreach-scope suite evaluates
(foreach e nil 99) by READ at run time and gets nil, status ok, on both
platforms.

THE ACCURATE STATEMENT:

    Loading a file that contains (foreach e nil 99) makes BricsCAD stop
    making progress -- no error, no further output -- while the same
    form evaluates correctly when READ at run time. Which mechanism
    stops it is not established.

That the fault is reached at form 3 and not before is solid: it rests
on markers that WERE written, not on ones that were not.

WHY THIS CORRECTION IS THE POINT AND NOT A FOOTNOTE: reading a silence
as evidence is the exact error that cost this investigation four wrong
guesses, and the instrumentation was built to stop it. It stopped it in
the MEASUREMENT -- the split legitimately narrows the fault to one
fragment -- and then it crept back into the CONCLUSION, where "did not
return" became "refused" became "will not read". The tooling was
sound; the sentence was not.


WITHDRAWN: (foreach e nil 99) IS NOT THE CULPRIT -- pjb, 2026-09-06
--------------------------------------------------------------------

pjb, at a BricsCAD V26 REPL on macOS:

    : (foreach e nil 99)
    nil

    (defun f () (foreach e nil 99))
    (f)                                  "definitely loaded successfully"

So the form reads, evaluates, survives being a DEFUN body, and the
function calls. Every step this ticket accused it of. THE
IDENTIFICATION IS WRONG and is withdrawn.

WHAT IT WAS BUILT ON, which is the lesson: fragment 003's load produced
no marker, fragment 003 contained that form, therefore that form was
the culprit. Two orderings agreed, which felt like corroboration. But
both readings rest on an ABSENCE plus two deductions -- which load came
next (from the wrapper) and what was in it (from the fragment file) --
and an absence supports no conclusion about a CAUSE. It says only that
something stopped.

WHAT STILL STANDS, because it rests on markers that WERE written:

  - fragments 001 and 002 load and their markers name what they defined
  - after the marker for the fragment before it, nothing further is
    ever written
  - the paths differ only in filename; the bytes are clean ASCII, no CR
  - the two runs stopped at the fragment holding that form in BOTH
    orderings, so the stop CORRELATES with it

Correlation with a form that is independently proven harmless means the
cause is somewhere the correlation does not reach: the CI context. The
candidates now are the ones the REPL test does not cover --

  - batch mode under alfe, versus an interactive session
  - the accumulated state of two prior LOADs, versus a fresh REPL
  - a modal dialog appearing for an unrelated reason (pjb has reported
    BricsCAD dialogs about a drawing being in use on this very runner),
    which stops everything and writes nothing

HARNESS CHANGE, so the next run needs one deduction fewer: each
fragment is now announced BEFORE its load as well as after, with the
PATH in the record. "entering <file>" followed by nothing then states
that the load was ENTERED and did not return, instead of leaving that
to be inferred from the wrapper.

AND THE FILE PATH IS EXONERATED TOO -- pjb, same day, with a .scr:

    (load "~/src/ogamita/clautolisp/probes/test-foreach.lsp")
    (setq log (open ".../test-foreach.out" "w"))
    (write-line "SUCCESS" log)
    (close log)
    ._QUIT

and the .out file contained SUCCESS. So the form LOADS FROM A FILE, in
BATCH, under a script -- the one axis the REPL test had left open, and
the axis this ticket was named after. The title is now wrong twice
over: BricsCAD neither refuses the form nor refuses to load it.

WHAT IS LEFT is the difference between that .scr and the CI run, since
the form, the defun, the file and batch mode are all cleared:

  - driven by ALFE (--bricscad --mode batch), not a bare .scr
  - the load is wrapped in VL-CATCH-ALL-APPLY
  - it is the third LOAD of the session, after probe-core and one or
    two fragments, not the first
  - a drawing is open, and pjb has reported BricsCAD modal dialogs
    about a drawing in use ON THIS RUNNER -- a dialog stops everything
    and writes nothing, which is precisely the signature seen
  - SECURELOAD is set to 0 by the wrapper

THE CORRELATION THAT MISLED ME, stated so it is not re-derived: the
stop tracked the empty-list FRAGMENT across two orderings, at two
different load positions. That looked like content-dependence and is
not, since the content is now cleared four ways. Either the correlation
is coincidence across two runs, or it is with the FILE rather than the
form -- which is why the byte-exact 184-byte fragment has been sent to
pjb to load directly. If that file loads for him, the fragment is
cleared too and the cause is wholly in the CI context.
